a-end="242">Here’s a proposed <strong data-start="18" data-end="51">Table of Contents for Part IV</strong>, designed to follow naturally after the implementation and scaling focus of Part III. It shifts from technical frameworks into the philosophical, ethical, and empirical consequences of RSVP:</p>
<hr data-start="244" data-end="247">
<h2 data-start="249" data-end="289"><strong data-start="252" data-end="289">Part IV: Implications and Futures</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="291" data-end="347"><strong data-start="295" data-end="347">Chapter 11. Philosophical Horizons of the Plenum</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="348" data-end="592">
<li data-start="348" data-end="433">
<p data-start="350" data-end="433">RSVP as a metaphysical framework: matter, mind, and meaning as continuous fields.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="434" data-end="508">
<p data-start="436" data-end="508">Connections to Ortega y Gasset, process philosophy, and phenomenology.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="509" data-end="592">
<p data-start="511" data-end="592">Rethinking individuality: persons as field nodes rather than isolated entities.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="594" data-end="650"><strong data-start="598" data-end="650">Chapter 12. Ethics and Political Economy in RSVP</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="651" data-end="858">
<li data-start="651" data-end="706">
<p data-start="653" data-end="706">Entropic ethics: coherence as a measure of justice.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="707" data-end="783">
<p data-start="709" data-end="783">Colonial and patriarchal legacies reinterpreted through field imbalance.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="784" data-end="858">
<p data-start="786" data-end="858">Toward cognitive sovereignty: protecting agency as a scalar potential.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="860" data-end="913"><strong data-start="864" data-end="913">Chapter 13. Empirical Predictions and Testing</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="914" data-end="1196">
<li data-start="914" data-end="1010">
<p data-start="916" data-end="1010">Observable cosmological predictions (CMB anomalies, redshift patterns, structure formation).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1011" data-end="1088">
<p data-start="1013" data-end="1088">Neurocognitive predictions (fMRI coherence, entropy in conscious states).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1089" data-end="1196">
<p data-start="1091" data-end="1196">Social-scientific predictions (stability of geozotic families, resilience of futarchy-like governance).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="1198" data-end="1250"><strong data-start="1202" data-end="1250">Chapter 14. RSVP and Artificial Intelligence</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1251" data-end="1432">
<li data-start="1251" data-end="1315">
<p data-start="1253" data-end="1315">HYDRA and TARTAN as architectures for distributed cognition.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1316" data-end="1366">
<p data-start="1318" data-end="1366">RSVP futarchy as governance for AI deployment.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1367" data-end="1432">
<p data-start="1369" data-end="1432">Ethical alignment: field coherence as an alignment criterion.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="1434" data-end="1490"><strong data-start="1438" data-end="1490">Chapter 15. Futures of Society and Consciousness</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1491" data-end="1696">
<li data-start="1491" data-end="1563">
<p data-start="1493" data-end="1563">Post-individualist attractor states: life beyond the founder burden.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1564" data-end="1631">
<p data-start="1566" data-end="1631">Utopian vs. dystopian trajectories of RSVP-informed governance.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1632" data-end="1696">
<p data-start="1634" data-end="1696">Final reflections: the fall of space, the rise of coherence.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1698" data-end="1701">
<p data-start="1703" data-end="1757">This would give you a <strong data-start="1725" data-end="1748">four-part structure</strong> overall:</p>
<ul data-start="1759" data-end="2073">
<li data-start="1759" data-end="1805">
<p data-start="1761" data-end="1805"><strong data-start="1761" data-end="1772">Part I:</strong> Foundations (theory + history)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1806" data-end="1872">
<p data-start="1808" data-end="1872"><strong data-start="1808" data-end="1820">Part II:</strong> Theoretical synthesis (math + category + entropy)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1873" data-end="1974">
<p data-start="1875" data-end="1974"><strong data-start="1875" data-end="1888">Part III:</strong> Implementation and scaling (HYDRA, TARTAN, futarchy, geozotic families, simulation)</p>
</li>
